  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/5f/Fantastic_Four_Annual_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The second story in the annual is more or less a retelling of the first four pages of last December's [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1] (story 2, really just pages 2-4, if one excludes the splash page), showing Spider-Man's first encounter with the Fantastic Four.&nbsp; Since the general outcome is the same, I won't bother to provide a detailed plot summary.\n\nRefer to the original issue to get the gist.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A modestly engaging redraw by Kirby that adds breadth without adding depth.\nInteresting enough as a pleasant revisit, but still fundamentally derivative.",
  "subtitle": "Spider-Man Meets the FF, Now With Extra Panels",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=FOR THE RECORD:]\n[BR] This is a review of the second of the three stories found in this magazine.\n[BR] My review on the first story is posted elsewhere.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThis second story of the summer <i>Fantastic Four</i> annual is essentially a retelling of Spider-Man's first encounter with the FF (in the second story of [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1], published last December), but <i>with additional detail</i>, apparently in response to \"countless requests,\" and this time drawn by Jack Kirby (with inks by Steve Ditko).&nbsp; As the text indicates, it is presented \"by special arrangement with <i>Spider-Man</i> magazine...\"\n\n[ASSET: right | CrummyPitcher | width=22%] Expanded from the original three pages to six, about the only thing Kirby does is devote a little extra space to Spider-Man's interactions with each of the Fantastic Four members in turn, adding between three and seven new panels of detail to each encounter.\n[BR]\n[BR] Of course, it is ever convenient for the other members of the FF to stand around and watch idly as each one of them takes their own individual crack at the spider-themed intruder.&nbsp; This was not as obvious in the original, where only two panels were devoted to each of those encounters, but the expanded space makes it far easier to imagine the others just standing there watching [SPAN: severity=warning; text=(<i>not</i> a positive, by the way)].\n\n[ASSET: left | JacksRecreations | width=24%] The original devoted a total of eight panels to the one-on-one encounters, while this one expands it out to 33 before Reed finally steps in and asks Spider-Man what he actually wants.&nbsp; As for the dialogue in the original eight panels, it is pretty much the same as it was before (not word-for-word <i>identical</i>, but very close), and Jack does a good job recreating the original look where applicable.\n\nWhy Stan felt the need to redraw the story rather than reprint just the relevant original panels, I can only guess, but perhaps Jack asked for the chance to put his own spin on the character.&nbsp; (However, I remind readers that he just <i>got</i> his chance to draw Spider-Man in last month's <i>Strange Tales</i> annual -- [LINK:1963:ST_Annual_2_torch0|ST Annual 2|2026-11-01] -- where I already commented on Kirby's rendition of the character.)\n\nFor the record, Kirby still doesn't seem to be able to get Spider-Man's mask right.&nbsp; Note the cover where it looks like a checkerboard pattern, as opposed to how it appears on the Ditko-inked pages within.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt's no surprise that Marvel had gotten requests to re-present this first cross-title team-up in its ever-growing universe, and indeed, right after this, the first half of [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1] is <i>actually</i> reprinted to help fill out the last several pages of this annual.\n\nNormally, when a comic is reprinted, I don't feel the need to give it its own rating, but I decided to do so here for the following reasons:\n[HANG] * it is an <i>expanded</i> version of the original\n[HANG] * it is weighted only a <i>third</i> of a full sized book due to its brevity\n[HANG] * it was <i>redrawn</i> as opposed to being an honest-to-goodness reprint.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And we'll see this again later in the Silver Age in the closing sections of the <i>Silver Surfer</i> magazine -- but that's still several years away (1968)...]\n\nJack's expanded battle sequences give readers a better sense of where Spider-Man ranks as a hero in his own right, as the young hero very successfully holds his own against the foursome, especially this early in his career.&nbsp; (But as I mentioned above, the additional space also leaves one with the mental picture of each FF member standing idly by while Spider-Man battles them in turn -- not as easy to imagine when it was as concentrated as it was before).\n\n[ASSET: left | WillTheyMeetAgain | width=16%] My grade for the original story was 7.0, but that included the Chameleon portion as well, which boosted <i>its</i> total weight to 0.5.&nbsp; As this story only covers the opening part of that encounter (pages 2-4), and is, in large part, a reprint, it did not seem to merit the same treatment.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even with the additional material, it is still largely derivative, so it can't really exceed mid‑grade in the end, with most of its appeal resting on the novelty of seeing the artwork handled by a different person.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=Will Spider-Man meet the Fantastic Four again?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=That is, of course, the question this brief story leaves us with...]",
  "highlight": "Spider‑Man's first brush with the Fantastic Four gets a Kirby stretch -- more <i>panels</i> -- same story."
}